<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rana Kumbha</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 1433–1468</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Maharana Kumbha, Kumbhakarna of Mewar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rana Kumbha was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sisodia">Sisodia</a> ruler of Mewar from 1433 to 1468. He fought the sultans of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> and fortified his kingdom with many strongholds, above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumbhalgarh">Kumbhalgarh</a>. At Chittor he raised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijay-stambha">Vijay Stambha</a>, the Victory Tower, and he was a patron of music and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> learning. He was murdered by his son Udai Karan.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rana-kumbha.jpg" alt="Rana Kumbha" width="320" height="499" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Asiatic society of UK · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kumbha_of_Mewar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
